# Narrative overlays for parts A-F: part introductions, per-result question / takeaway, and figure placement.
PARTS = {
"A": ("Audit: compiled programs are fragile", "We start from the observation that motivated everything else: programs compiled into transformer weights by Tracr are exactly correct, but how much noise can their parameters tolerate? We measure six programs under several noise protocols, compare them with trained transformers of the same depth, and ask which programs are fragile and why."),
"B": ("Mechanism and scaling with sequence length", "Part A showed fragility; Part B asks how it depends on the maximum sequence length N and which weights are responsible. Counting (storing the count as 1/(n+1) in an attention average) is the extreme case, so most experiments use the `length` program."),
"C": ("Hardening Tracr-compiled models", "If compiled networks are fragile, can they be made tolerant without losing exactness? We try exact rescalings that leave the function unchanged, lowering the attention scale, adding snapping layers, noise-aware fine-tuning, sensitivity-balanced rescaling, and training against a certificate. The honest summary is a few-fold gain at best, and no change in the decay with length."),
"D": ("Digital (restoring-state) designs", "Instead of repairing analog readouts, we replace them: the state is a one-hot vector that is snapped back to a valid value at every step, so errors cannot accumulate. We compare designs on the same tasks, add a bilinear (second-order) form with no extra parameters and weight-tied composition of many programs, and compare with trained GRUs. **This family of constructions follows Omlin and Giles (1996); see Part M.**"),
"E": ("Compiler and certificates", "A restoring-state network can be accompanied by proofs. We build a small compiler from finite-state specifications, prove exactness for sequences of any length, certify a noise radius with interval bounds, and then attack the certificate to see how tight it is."),
"F": ("Zeros, crosstalk and composition", "Two structural questions about the digital designs: how much of the fragility comes from noise on the (exactly zero) unused weights, and what happens when several programs are combined in one network or when trained networks are merged."),
"G": ("Trained-transformer comparisons and hybrids", "We check whether trained transformers can also be made noise-tolerant (noise-aware training), how their tolerance decays with length, and whether bolting a frozen digital counter onto a transformer helps."),
"H": ("Wild ideas", "Three out-of-the-box ideas: replicate state units, superpose codes to compress state, and store several programs in one associative memory using sparse error-correcting codes. All were tried at small scale; the sparse-code idea was revisited in Part I."),
"I": ("Weight-tied attacks, error-correcting codes and a fast-weight universal interpreter", "The completion of a plan with three goals: (1) attack the weight-tied composition designs per head and jointly, (2) test error-correcting state codes under a full matrix of noise conditions, (3) build a *universal interpreter* whose slow weights are shared by all programs while each program lives only in fast weights written from a program tape. The central question of (3): does one shared interpreter give shared *capacity*, or only shared *execution*?"),
}
# per-subsection: (question, takeaway)
SUB = {
"A1": ("How fragile are six compiled Tracr programs under Gaussian noise on their weights, and which parameter groups matter?",
 "Noise of about 1e-4 to 1e-3 per parameter already breaks most programs, even though the compiled weights are exact and roughly 94 to 99 percent of them are zero. Noise on the MLP input weights almost never matters (sigma50 infinite for three programs), while embeddings and attention value/output limit the tolerance. `sort_freq` is already inexact at the smallest noise tested."),
"A2": ("Is the fragility a property of the transformer architecture, or of compilation?",
 "Trained transformers of the same depth tolerate relative noise 15 to 170 times larger than compiled ones, and removing LayerNorm does not change that. The fragility belongs to this *realization* of the computation, not to the architecture."),
"A3": ("Which programs are fragile? A static analysis of what each program contains, plus noise.",
 "Every fragile program contains an attention readout: counting through `SelectorWidth`, or plain numerical averaging. The one program with no attention (`map`) is 100 to 500 times more tolerant. Averaging-only programs are as fragile as histograms, so the accurate description is *precise analog readout from attention averages*, with counting as the extreme case. This is correlational evidence across programs."),
"A4": ("A control: do programs with and without counting differ?",
 "The per-token lookup has sigma50 around 0.11 to 0.15 and barely decays with length, against 1e-4 to 1e-3 for counting programs. The matching attention-without-counting control (shift by one) did not compile in Tracr, so the causal claim rests on the across-program comparison."),
"B1": ("How does counting robustness depend on the maximum length N?",
 "Tolerance falls from 1.4e-3 at N=4 to 4.4e-4 at N=8, and for N of 12 and above the default compiler is not even exact at zero noise because the discretizer sharpness is fixed. This silent loss of exactness is a compiler limitation we report; it matches an independent anecdotal report of Tracr instability for long sequences. Lowering the attention scale gives a roughly constant 3 times."),
"B2": ("How sharp must the compiler's discretizer be to stay exact at large N?",
 "Raising the sharpness S to about N squared restores exactness (S=300 up to N=16, S=1000 at N=24). Even then tolerance keeps falling, roughly as N to the minus 1.5, and over-sharp settings slightly reduce it."),
"B3": ("Is the length decay specific to the counting program?",
 "No. Tolerance falls with N for `reverse`, `hist`, `sort_unique` and `frac_prevs` too, with log-log slopes between about -0.4 and -1.2 at the default attention scale. The benefit of a smaller attention scale shrinks with N, and `frac_prevs` loses exactness at scale 0.25 and N=16."),
"B4": ("Which weights limit the `length` program?",
 "Embeddings alone nearly equal the all-weights tolerance, followed by the attention key and value paths; MLP input weights barely matter. The measured slopes (about -1.1 for embeddings, -1.5 for the value path) match a simple analysis in which the count is stored as 1/(n+1) and must be resolved to better than the gap between adjacent counts."),
"B5": ("Can counting inside short windows, then adding exact integers, remove the length dependence?",
 "It improves the attention-path components 4 to 7 times, but the extra MLP layers become the new bottleneck, so overall tolerance gains only about 1.2 times at large N and the trend with N is unchanged. The N=48 chunked runs did not finish."),
"C1": ("How much can exact, function-preserving rewrites of the compiled weights help?",
 "Rescaling alone gives 1.1 to 1.3 times; adding dense snap layers hurts (0.4 to 0.6 times) because they add noisy parameters; lowering the attention scale gives 3 to 4 times and the combination 4 to 8 times. One variant (`frac_prevs`, scale 0.1) is not exact and is excluded."),
"C2": ("Can noise-aware fine-tuning starting from the compiled weights fix it?",
 "From the raw compiled weights, fine-tuning destroys exactness (for example `reverse`: only 23,185 of 335,922 sequences correct); from the rescaled, lowered-attention-scale initialization it keeps exactness (verified exhaustively at N=8) and gives 4 to 19 times, at the price of densifying the weights to 84 to 92 percent non-zero."),
"C3": ("Can an optimizer find the best exact rescaling under noise (sensitivity balancing)?",
 "Optimizing only exact symmetries gives a constant 2.8 to 3.8 times gain at N=16 to 48 with no change in function (max output change about 5e-4), and the slope with N moves only from -1.6 to -1.4."),
"C4": ("Does balancing carry over to other programs?",
 "Balancing alone gives 2.7 to 6.3 times and, with attention scale 0.25, 7 to 13 times on five of six settings. The decay with N is unchanged."),
"C5": ("Can a network be trained to maximize a provable radius under a general interval bound?",
 "No. The general bound certifies radius 0 for the compiled weights, training never reached a certified radius, trained weights lost exactness on long sequences (length-400 accuracy 0.53 and 0.81) and tolerance got worse. A negative result."),
"D1": ("Same task, different designs: how does noise tolerance scale?",
 "A hand-built digital one-hot counter with per-step snapping has a slope of about -0.36; without snapping about -0.93; a linear-state accumulator decays as 1/N squared; the Tracr attention counter about -1.3 to -1.6. These are toy designs on slightly different tasks, so they show tendencies, not a ranking."),
"D2": ("Does the advantage hold on a richer causal program, the prefix histogram?",
 "The digital design with snapping is nearly flat in N (1.3 to 1.6e-2 from N=16 to 128); removing snapping gives about N to the minus 1.7; Tracr causal is about 1e-4 at N=32. The price: parameters grow as N squared."),
"D3": ("A second program: majority-so-far.",
 "The signed one-hot counter with snapping decays slowly (6.5e-2 to 2.1e-2 over N=8 to 64) and is exact on all 7,776 length-5 sequences; Tracr causal is 1.0e-3 at N=8 and below the smallest noise tested at N=32. Here the digital design is also smaller (about 44k against 505k parameters at N=32)."),
"D4": ("Is the digital advantage just redundancy? Compare with r copies of the Tracr model at equal parameter count.",
 "Redundancy helps Tracr (2 times at N=16, 6.6 times at N=32) but the digital design remains 11 to 28 times better at equal parameters."),
"D5": ("Does the conclusion survive the relative (scale-free) noise protocol?",
 "Yes. alpha50 of the digital prefix histogram is 0.107, 0.081, 0.072, 0.061 at N=8, 16, 32, 64, comparable to early under-trained transformers."),
"D6": ("Can snap thresholds and a global scale be tuned to improve the digital designs?",
 "No: the best gains are 1.0 times and 1.1 times; the defaults are already near-optimal for these knobs. A negative result."),
"D7": ("Replace four dense snap matrices by a weight-free clip activation.",
 "20 to 25 percent fewer parameters, still exact, and 1.7 to 1.9 times higher sigma50 (1.2 to 1.5 times in relative alpha50). Removing noisy parameters from the restoring step helps."),
"D8": ("A bilinear (second-order) form, and weight-tied composition of many programs.",
 "The bilinear form z = sum over tokens of W_v s has no gates, biases or snap matrices; relative tolerance rises from about 0.1 to 0.6 to 1.0 and stays flat to N=64. The tied variant shares transition matrices between template programs: 30 exact programs fit in about 4,000 parameters with flat per-head tolerance. Note the bilinear form is the second-order recurrent network of Omlin and Giles; the tied design only works for programs that are instances of one template."),
"D9": ("Combine tied and bilinear.",
 "A role-gated multiplicative update with no hidden gates or biases: about 3,400 to 3,900 parameters for 1 to 30 exact programs, per-head sigma50 about 0.12 regardless of k."),
"D10": ("Matched comparison with trained GRUs: same programs, distribution, evaluation sequences and noise protocols.",
 "The bilinear design is competitive with small trained GRUs but not uniformly better: on majority it is below them at N=16 and above them at N=32 (where the GRUs decay); on dyck the GRUs are 1.2 to 1.9 times higher in sigma50. One token distribution per task and six noise draws."),
"D11": ("Trained GRU baselines.",
 "GRU(128) does not learn the prefix histogram exactly at N=32 in 3,000 steps (clean 0.58 to 0.71) but learns majority exactly at all N; GRU(256), 7,000 steps, reaches 0.995 and 0.98 accuracy at N=16 and 32 with alpha50 of 0.43 and 0.20."),
"E1": ("A compiler from finite-state specifications to a restoring-state network, with exactness and noise certificates.",
 "Every transition is verified exactly (valid for any length by induction) and a certified radius is computed by interval bounds; empirical tolerance decays about N to the minus 0.45. Omlin and Giles (1996) compile automata into recurrent networks with stability proofs, so this is not a new compiler concept; the contribution here is measurement."),
"E2": ("Attack the certificate: is it tight?",
 "All programs hold at 1 times the certified radius and break at 1.1 times, so the ReLU-snap certificate is tight. An earlier attack with a mismatched tube width suggested the opposite and is superseded."),
"E3": ("How does the certified radius scale with the number of states?",
 "When all entries may be perturbed, the radius falls roughly as D to the minus 0.4 (majority 3.3e-3 to 1.5e-3 and dyck 3.5e-3 to 2.1e-3 from N=8 to 64)."),
"E4": ("What if exactly-zero weights are protected from noise?",
 "The certified radius is then constant in network size (4.03e-3 majority, 3.92e-3 dyck, N=8 to 64): the size dependence of the dense case is entirely crosstalk through the zero entries. The attack again holds at 1 times and breaks at 1.1 times."),
"E5": ("Certificates for the bilinear and weight-tied designs.",
 "Radii are 15 to 40 times larger than the ReLU-snap design; the tied certificate is valid for any number of instances. Caveats: the zero-protected value may be limited by the tube grid, and the bilinear majority certificate could not be broken by the attack even at 1.5 times (conservative there). Superseded in value by E6."),
"E6": ("Tighten the certificates and attack with a function-level adversary.",
 "Searching the tube width finely gives bilinear dense radii of 0.068, 0.057, 0.046 (majority) and 0.077, 0.067, 0.057 (dyck) for N=8, 16, 32, and exactly 0.100 when zeros are protected (an analytic limit from the saturating rows of the shift matrices). A real wrong output is found at 2 times the certified radius for every dense case and at 2 to 3 times for zero-protected cases, so the certificates are within about 2 to 3 times of a demonstrated counterexample."),
"E7": ("Extend the function-level attack to N=32 and to the tied design with k=1, 4, 15.",
 "At 1 times the certified radius the network is still correct (margin 0.89 to 0.92) in all ten cases; wrong outputs appear at 2 times (dense) and 2 to 3 times (protected). Results are identical for k=1, 4 and 15, so the tied certificate and its tightness do not depend on k. The k=15 run died once and was resumed with the same script."),
"F1": ("How much fragility comes from the exactly-zero entries?",
 "Protecting zeros gains about 1.5 to 2.9 times for digital designs and 1.9 to 2.3 times for Tracr, roughly constant in N: zeros are not what drives Tracr's decay with length."),
"F2": ("Parallel composition of k compiled programs in one dense network.",
 "All composites are exact (certificate 1224/1224 at k=8), parameters grow roughly as k squared and joint tolerance falls about k to the minus 0.65."),
"F3": ("Composition: per-head tolerance and composite certificates.",
 "With zeros protected, per-head sigma50 is flat in k (7.6e-2 to 8.7e-2) and the certified radius is the same for k=1, 2, 4 (about 3.9e-3 to 4.0e-3); with dense noise both fall. The decay of the joint number is largely the statistical cost of requiring every head to be right."),
"F4": ("Composing trained GRUs: merging weights vs stacking blocks.",
 "Averaging weights destroys both programs (accuracy 0.04 and 0.15). Block-diagonal stacking is exact with no tolerance cost (alpha50 about 1). Block-diagonal composition is easy in any architecture; the open problem is shared-capacity composition (see Part I)."),
}
